  <div class="wrap">
<div class="hub-sub">Extended reference poster · Chinese GAAP (ASBE / CAS) · 存货 · Substantially converged with IAS 2 · RMB examples</div></div></div>



<div class="section-header" id="s1"><span class="num">1</span><h2>Conceptual Framework Linkage</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">One of the earliest and most closely converged ASBEs</div><div class="principle">ASBE No. 1 governs the RECOGNITION, MEASUREMENT, and DISCLOSURE of INVENTORIES — assets held for SALE in the ordinary course of business, in the PROCESS of production for such sale, or in the form of MATERIALS or supplies to be CONSUMED in production or the RENDERING of services. This is ONE of the FOUNDATIONAL specific standards issued alongside the 2006 ASBE overhaul, and REMAINS substantially CONVERGED with IAS 2 internationally.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s2"><span class="num">2</span><h2>Scope</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">All enterprises holding inventories for sale, production, or consumption</div><div class="principle">This Standard applies to ALL enterprises with INVENTORIES WITHIN its scope — EXCLUDING work-in-progress arising under CONSTRUCTION contracts (governed by ASBE No. 15), financial instruments, and the BIOLOGICAL assets addressed separately by ASBE No. 5, WHERE those items fall OUTSIDE the general inventory definition.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s3"><span class="num">3</span><h2>Initial Measurement — Cost Components</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">Purchase cost, processing cost, and other costs to bring inventory to its present condition</div>
<div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1fr 2fr;"><span>Cost component</span><span>Description</span></div>
<div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1fr 2fr;"><span>Purchase cost</span><span>Purchase price, related taxes, transport, loading/unloading, and other directly attributable costs, LESS trade discounts and rebates</span></div>
<div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1fr 2fr;"><span>Processing cost</span><span>Direct labour and a SYSTEMATIC allocation of production overheads incurred in converting materials into finished goods</span></div>
<div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1fr 2fr;"><span>Other costs</span><span>Other expenditures incurred in bringing the inventory to its PRESENT location and condition</span></div>
</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s4"><span class="num">4</span><h2>Cost Formulas — No LIFO Permitted</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cs span2"><div class="ctitle cs">Weighted average, FIFO, or specific identification only</div><div style="font-size:11px;color:var(--color-text-secondary);line-height:1.6;">For inventory items that are NOT ordinarily interchangeable, or goods/services PRODUCED and SEGREGATED for specific projects, the SPECIFIC IDENTIFICATION method is used. For OTHER inventories, the WEIGHTED AVERAGE COST or FIRST-IN, FIRST-OUT (FIFO) formula is applied — the LAST-IN, FIRST-OUT (LIFO) formula is NOT PERMITTED, CONSISTENT with IAS 2's own prohibition internationally.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s5"><span class="num">5</span><h2>Subsequent Measurement — Lower of Cost and Net Realisable Value</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">The core measurement principle</div><div style="font-size:11px;color:var(--color-text-secondary);line-height:1.6;">At each Balance Sheet date, inventories are measured at the LOWER of COST and NET REALISABLE VALUE (NRV) — WHERE NRV has fallen BELOW cost, a PROVISION for inventory write-down (存货跌价准备) is recognised, with the CORRESPONDING charge recognised in the Income Statement.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s6"><span class="num">6</span><h2>Determining Net Realisable Value</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cs span2"><div class="ctitle cs">Estimated selling price, less costs to complete and sell</div><div style="font-size:11px;color:var(--color-text-secondary);line-height:1.6;">NET REALISABLE VALUE is the ESTIMATED selling price in the ORDINARY course of business, LESS the estimated costs of COMPLETION and the estimated costs NECESSARY to make the SALE — a forward-looking, MARKET-based estimate REQUIRING regular reassessment WHENEVER conditions change.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s7"><span class="num">7</span><h2>Reversal of Inventory Write-Downs — Permitted</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">Where the circumstances causing the write-down no longer exist</div><div style="font-size:11px;color:var(--color-text-secondary);line-height:1.6;">WHERE the CIRCUMSTANCES that PREVIOUSLY caused an inventory write-down NO LONGER exist (e.g. the market price of the inventory has RECOVERED), the WRITE-DOWN is REVERSED — LIMITED to the AMOUNT of the ORIGINAL write-down — with the REVERSAL recognised as a REDUCTION in the current period's INVENTORY write-down expense. This REVERSAL is PERMITTED, CONSISTENT with IAS 2's own approach internationally.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s8"><span class="num">8</span><h2>Contrast With ASBE 8's No-Reversal Rule for Other Assets</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">A genuinely different rule applies to long-lived assets</div><div style="font-size:11px;color:var(--color-text-secondary);line-height:1.6;">This REVERSAL permission is DISTINCT from the treatment of LONG-LIVED asset impairment under ASBE No. 8 (Impairment of Assets — see the companion poster), which PROHIBITS the reversal of impairment losses ONCE recognised for fixed assets, intangible assets, and GOODWILL. INVENTORIES are NOT subject to this NO-REVERSAL rule — a genuinely IMPORTANT distinction for PREPARERS to keep straight, since the TWO standards apply OPPOSITE reversal principles to DIFFERENT asset categories.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s9"><span class="num">9</span><h2>Worked Example — Applying the Lower of Cost and NRV Rule</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">Recognising a write-down when NRV falls below cost</div>
<div class="worked" style="margin-bottom:0;"><div class="wk-title">Example: A finished goods inventory with a falling market price</div><div class="wk-body">A company holds finished goods inventory with a cost of RMB 5,000,000. Due to a market downturn, the estimated selling price is now RMB 4,600,000, with estimated selling costs of RMB 200,000.</div><div class="wk-answer">Net realisable value = RMB 4,600,000 − RMB 200,000 = <strong>RMB 4,400,000</strong>. Since NRV (RMB 4,400,000) is BELOW cost (RMB 5,000,000), the company recognises an inventory write-down of RMB 5,000,000 − RMB 4,400,000 = <strong>RMB 600,000</strong>, measuring the inventory at its LOWER NRV amount.</div></div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s10"><span class="num">10</span><h2>Worked Example — Reversing a Prior Write-Down</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">Recovering value up to (but not beyond) the original cost</div>
<div class="worked" style="margin-bottom:0;"><div class="wk-title">Example: A market price recovery in the following period</div><div class="wk-body">Continuing the prior example, in the following period, market prices recover, and the SAME inventory's net realisable value rises to RMB 4,800,000.</div><div class="wk-answer">The company REVERSES the write-down, but ONLY up to the ORIGINAL cost of RMB 5,000,000 — recognising a reversal of RMB 5,000,000 − RMB 4,400,000 = <strong>RMB 600,000</strong> (the FULL amount of the original write-down), REDUCING the current period's inventory write-down expense, WITHOUT recognising the inventory ABOVE its original RMB 5,000,000 cost.</div></div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s11"><span class="num">11</span><h2>Disclosure Requirements</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">What ASBE No. 1 requires</div><div class="check-list"><span>Accounting policies adopted for inventories, including the cost formula used</span><span>Carrying amount of inventories, by classification</span><span>Amount of any inventory write-down recognised, and any reversal, as expense for the period</span><span>Carrying amount of inventories pledged as security for liabilities</span></div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s12"><span class="num">12</span><h2>Critical Judgments &amp; Estimates</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cn span2"><div class="ctitle cn">Where judgement matters most</div>
<div class="two-col"><div><div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT JUDGEMENTS</div><div class="dot-list"><span>Whether an item is "ordinarily interchangeable," determining if specific identification or a cost formula applies</span></div></div>
<div><div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT ESTIMATES</div><div class="dot-list"><span>Estimated selling price and costs to complete/sell, underlying the net realisable value calculation</span><span>Allocation basis for production overheads included in processing cost</span></div></div></div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s13"><span class="num">13</span><h2>Relationship to IAS 2 and US GAAP</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">Comparison — ASBE 1 vs IAS 2 vs US GAAP (ASC 330)</div>
<div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1.1fr 1fr 1fr 1fr;"><span>Feature</span><span style="color:var(--color-text-warning);">ASBE 1</span><span style="color:var(--color-text-info);">IAS 2</span><span style="color:var(--color-text-success);">US GAAP</span></div>
<div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.1fr 1fr 1fr 1fr;"><span>Measurement basis</span><span>Lower of cost and NRV</span><span>Lower of cost and NRV — converged</span><span>Lower of cost and NRV (or market, for LIFO/retail method users)</span></div>
<div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.1fr 1fr 1fr 1fr;"><span>LIFO permitted</span><span>NOT permitted</span><span>NOT permitted — converged</span><span>PERMITTED — a genuine US GAAP-specific divergence</span></div>
<div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.1fr 1fr 1fr 1fr;"><span>Write-down reversal</span><span>PERMITTED, up to original cost</span><span>PERMITTED, up to original cost — converged</span><span>NOT permitted once recognised</span></div>
</div>
<div class="note">ASBE 1 is one of the MOST thoroughly converged standards with IAS 2 — the MEANINGFUL divergence here is between IFRS/ASBE on ONE side and US GAAP on the OTHER (LIFO availability, write-down reversal), rather than between China and the international standard.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s14"><span class="num">14</span><h2>Statement Mapping</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cs span2"><div class="ctitle cs">Where every inventory event lands</div>
<div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Event</span><span>Statement(s) affected</span><span>Notes</span></div>
<div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Inventory carrying amount</span><span><span class="badge bs">BS</span></span><span>At the lower of cost and net realisable value</span></div>
<div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Write-down / reversal</span><span><span class="badge bp">IS</span></span><span>Recognised within cost of sales or a separate write-down expense line</span></div>
</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s15"><span class="num">15</span><h2>Related Standards</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">Cross-reference map</div>
<div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>Standard</span><span>Connection</span></div>
<div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>ASBE No. 8</span><span>Impairment of Assets — governs the DIFFERENT, no-reversal treatment applicable to fixed assets, intangibles, and goodwill</span></div>
<div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>ASBE No. 15</span><span>Construction Contracts — governs work-in-progress arising under construction contracts, outside this Standard's scope</span></div>
</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s16"><span class="num">16</span><h2>Quick-Reference Glossary</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cn span2">
<div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Net realisable value (NRV)</div><div class="glossary-def">The estimated selling price in the ordinary course of business, less estimated costs of completion and costs necessary to make the sale.</div></div>
<div class="glossary-item" style="border-bottom:none;"><div class="glossary-term">Inventory write-down provision (存货跌价准备)</div><div class="glossary-def">A provision recognised when an inventory item's net realisable value falls below its cost, reducing the inventory's carrying amount to NRV.</div></div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="legend"><div class="ltitle">Financial statement key (Chinese GAAP / ASBE terminology)</div><div class="lrow"><span class="badge bs">BS</span> Balance Sheet (资产负债表) <span class="badge bp">IS</span> Income Statement (利润表) <span class="badge bo">SCOE</span> Statement of Changes in Owners' Equity (所有者权益变动表) <span class="badge bc">CFS</span> Cash Flow Statement (现金流量表)</div></div>


</div>
  </div>
  <section id="history">
    <HistoryTimeline items={history.asbe01 || []} />
  </section>
</StandardPageLayout>
